From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: Clients open their own files; the server stops opening them for others

Wine's server opened files on its clients' behalf: NtCreateFile sent a name,
and open_fd() called open() in the server. With the shared machine-level
wineserver (patches 0001, 0004, 0005) running as SYSTEM for every user, the
kernel therefore checked SYSTEM's rights for every user's every open. Measured
in the Stained Glass image as an ordinary user: a Windows program created a file
in C:\windows\system32 and read a SYSTEM-only (0600) file -- both refused to the
same user by Unix -- and could not write the user's own 0700 directory. A
privilege escalation (Stained Glass ADR 0013, multi-user debt D14).

Now the client opens -- and creates -- the file itself, as its own Unix user,
and sends the fd with the create_file request; the server adopts it and opens
nothing, so the kernel has checked the real user's rights. The server opens by
name only for clients running as its own Unix account, and refuses everyone
else (STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED): without that refusal a client could simply omit
the fd.

  - ntdll: open_unix_file() opens with the flags the server used for each
    disposition, the same access-to-O_RDWR/O_WRONLY/O_RDONLY mapping, the same
    read-only retry for directories, and the server's errno-to-status mapping
    (ntdll's own differs, and NtCreateFile's callers see these statuses). A
    file it creates starts 0600 and gets the server's computed mode (from the
    security descriptor or the default) under the process umask once the
    server answers, so it is never briefly readable by others.
  - server: open_fd() takes the client's fd and skips mkdir()/open(); the
    sharing check, truncation and inode bookkeeping are unchanged. The fd's
    Unix name comes from /proc/self/fd, not from the requested path: the server
    may be unable to traverse the path, and must not take it on the client's
    word, since the name is what delete-on-close and rename act on.
  - server: reopening a file object by name (ReOpenFile) would also run with
    the server's rights, so it is refused for other users' clients until it is
    done client-side.
  - protocol: create_file carries unix_fd; its reply carries the mode.

Not covered here, and still performed with the server's rights: unlinking a
delete-on-close file and renaming (fd.c close path and set_fd_name), and
device nodes opened by the server (device.c). Those follow in later patches.

Forwarded: not-needed
